      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "a0z0",
      "autoResize": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ovBody",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -760,
      "y": 64,
      "width": 700,
      "height": 980,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1063,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 100063,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785200000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Most currency unions look like the euro: a political project\nthat hands every member a single shared currency and a single\ncentral bank. 301stablecoin asked a narrower, more market-\ndriven question — could you get some of the stability benefits\nof a regional currency without the political machinery, by\nissuing a blockchain token backed by a basket of the region's\nown FIAT currencies?\n\nThe thesis was regional. Instead of one global stablecoin, the\nbasket would be scoped to a bloc — the MENA region was the\nprimary focus, with ASEAN and LATAM as further candidates.\nWithin a bloc, weaker and more volatile currencies could lean\non the stability of stronger neighbours, while the stronger\neconomies gained a safer, lower-friction unit to trade with the\nweaker ones — less currency risk, lower conversion cost, and\nideally more cross-border commerce as a result.\n\nThe project had two halves. The first was an off-chain\nstatistical study: a numpy / pandas notebook that ran over the\nhistorical series of every currency in the basket, scoring each\non stability across a wide parameter set and over both short\nand long horizons. Those scores were then translated into a\nrecommended weight per currency. The method was adapted\nfrom an econometrics paper out of the University of Pavia, re-\ntuned because MENA currencies swing far more than the ones\nthe original paper was built around.\n\nThe second half was the smart contract. An admin-only method\npushed the updated weights on-chain. From there a set of\nquadratic incentives did the balancing work: users were\nrewarded for supplying currencies the basket held below their\ntarget weight and penalised on over-represented ones —\nnudging on-chain reserves toward the desired composition\nwithout an operator manually rebalancing.\n\nIt's worth being honest about scope. The statistical side was\nreal, working code, validated on historical data. The smart\ncontract was designed on paper — incentives and all — but\nnever deployed. It also assumed that pegged stablecoin\nvariants of these FIAT currencies were already liquid and\nready to compose into a basket, which at the time simply\nwasn't true. It was an R&D bet on a future that hadn't arrived\nyet — and nobody had seriously attempted it, which was\nprecisely the point.",
      "fontSize": 17.7,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Most currency unions look like the euro: a political project that hands every member a single shared currency and a single central bank. 301stablecoin asked a narrower, more market-driven question — could you get some of the stability benefits of a regional currency without the political machinery, by issuing a blockchain token backed by a basket of the region's own FIAT currencies?\n\nThe thesis was regional. Instead of one global stablecoin, the basket would be scoped to a bloc — the MENA region was the primary focus, with ASEAN and LATAM as further candidates. Within a bloc, weaker and more volatile currencies could lean on the stability of stronger neighbours, while the stronger economies gained a safer, lower-friction unit to trade with the weaker ones — less currency risk, lower conversion cost, and ideally more cross-border commerce as a result.\n\nThe project had two halves. The first was an off-chain statistical study: a numpy / pandas notebook that ran over the historical series of every currency in the basket, scoring each on stability across a wide parameter set and over both short and long horizons. Those scores were then translated into a recommended weight per currency. The method was adapted from an econometrics paper out of the University of Pavia, re-tuned because MENA currencies swing far more than the ones the original paper was built around.\n\nThe second half was the smart contract. An admin-only method pushed the updated weights on-chain. From there a set of quadratic incentives did the balancing work: users were rewarded for supplying currencies the basket held below their target weight and penalised on over-represented ones — nudging on-chain reserves toward the desired composition without an operator manually rebalancing.\n\nIt's worth being honest about scope. The statistical side was real, working code, validated on historical data. The smart contract was designed on paper — incentives and all — but never deployed. It also assumed that pegged stablecoin variants of these FIAT currencies were already liquid and ready to compose into a basket, which at the time simply wasn't true. It was an R&D bet on a future that hadn't arrived yet — and nobody had seriously attempted it, which was precisely the point.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "a0z1",
      "autoResize": false
    }
  ],
